A presidential election was held in Portugal on 23 January 2011. Incumbent head of state [[Aníbal Cavaco Silva]] ran for a second term as [[President of Portugal|president]], facing a rematch by [[Manuel Alegre]], this time with the support of the PS and also the [[Left Bloc]] (BE).

Cavaco Silva won re-election by a landslide, with 53 percent of the votes, winning all 18 districts, both autonomous regions of [[Azores]] and [[Madeira]] and 292 [[Municipalities of Portugal|municipalities]] out of a total of 308. Manuel Alegre polled once again second, but below his 2006 result, with just 19.7 percent of the votes and polling first in just 3 municipalities, whereas independent candidate [[Fernando Nobre]] finished a strong third with 14 percent of the votes. The communist supported candidate, [[Francisco Lopes]], achieved a rather strong result, taking into account his low recognition in the electorate, by gathering 7 percent of the votes and taking first place in 10 municipalities,<ref>{{cite news |url=https://sol.iol.pt/2011/01/23/francisco-lopes-reclama-ja-um-bom-resultado |title=Francisco Lopes reclama já um bom resultado |language=pt |work=Sol |access-date=26 August 2026 |date=23 January 2011}}</ref>, while [[novelty candidate]] [[José Manuel Coelho]] surprised with 4.5 percent of the votes, having performed well in his native [[Madeira]].<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.marktest.com/wap/a/n/id~16ef.aspx |title=A geografia das Presidenciais de 2011 |language=pt |work=Marktest |access-date=26 August 2026 |date=25 January 2011}}</ref>

Turnout in this election was very low, with only 46.52 percent of the electorate casting a ballot, the lowest till then (only surpassed in 2021). Cavaco Silva was sworn in for a second term as President on 9 March 2011.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://sicnoticias.pt/arquivo/presidenciais2011/2011-03-09-cavaco-silva-toma-posse-para-segundo-mandato6 |title=Cavaco Silva toma posse para segundo mandato |language=pt |work=SIC Notícias |access-date=18 February 2026 |date=9 March 2011}}</ref>

==Background==
In the [[2006 Portuguese presidential election|2006 presidential election]], former Prime Minister Aníbal Cavaco Silva, the only candidate of the center-right, was elected on the first round with 50.5 percent of the votes.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2006-01-31 |title=Resultados oficiais dão vitória a Cavaco Silva com 50,54 por cento |url=https://www.publico.pt/2006/01/31/politica/noticia/resultados-oficiais-dao-vitoria-a-cavaco-silva-com-5054-por-cento-1246441 |access-date=2026-08-26 |website=Público |language=pt}}</ref>  He had faced two particular candidates from the ruling [[Socialist Party (Portugal)|Socialist Party]], the official candidate [[Mário Soares]], former President of the Republic came in third with 14.3 percent, and Manuel Alegre, a dissident, who ranked second with 20.7 percent of votes. This historic victory of a conservative candidate, the first after the Carnation Revolution, inaugurated a period of "political cohabitation" with Socialist Prime Minister [[José Sócrates]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2006-12-28 |title=2006, "ano um" da coabitação Cavaco-Sócrates e das "manifs" contra o Governo  |url=https://www.rtp.pt/noticias/pais/2006-ano-um-da-coabitacao-cavaco-socrates-e-das-manifs-contra-o-governo_n38319 |access-date=2026-08-26 |website=RTP |language=pt}}</ref> In the beginning, the relationship between Cavaco and Sócrates was good, but by the middle of the president's first term, it became more tense, reaching a breaking point in 2009.<ref name="CSJS06">{{Cite web |date=2017-02-16 |title=Cavaco e Sócrates: história de tensões numa relação difícil  |url=https://www.publico.pt/2017/02/16/politica/noticia/historia-de-tensoes-numa-relacao-dificil-1762142 |access-date=2026-08-26 |website=Público |language=pt}}</ref> 

The president started warning about the country's economic situation, mainly [[unemployment]], and also disagreeing with the government regarding legislation.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2017-02-17 |title=Sócrates e Cavaco: da lua de mel à lua de fel |url=https://expresso.pt/politica/2017-02-16-Socrates-e-Cavaco-da-lua-de-mel-a-lua-de-fel |access-date=2026-08-26 |website=Expresso |language=pt}}</ref> Plus, just before the [[2009 Portuguese legislative election|September 2009 legislative election]], which returned the PS to power, albeit under a minority capacity, the presidency started to suspect that they were being monitored by the government through [[Wiretapping|wiretaps]].<ref>[https://www.publico.pt/2009/09/30/politica/noticia/cronologia-do-caso-das-escutas-1403009 Cronologia do caso das escutas], ''Público'', 30 September 2009. Retrieved 26 August 2026.</ref> Furthermore, a banking scandal that led to the nationalization of a [[Banco Português de Negócios|bank]] managed by figures close to the president also created more friction between Cavaco Silva and prime minister José Sócrates.<ref>{{citation | title =José Sócrates responde a Cavaco Silva e recusa "luta política" paga por "inocentes  | url= https://www.publico.pt/2011/01/08/jornal/jose-socrates-responde-a-cavaco-silva-e-recusa-luta-politica-paga-por-inocentes-20975365 | work = Público | date= 8 January 2011| access-date = 26 August 2026}}.</ref> By the same time, the country's economic situation worsened, following the [[2008 financial crisis]] and the [[Euro area crisis]], with the government being forced to adopt austerity measures and budget cuts, leading the head of state to intervene and mediate talks between the government and the PSD opposition,<ref name="CSJS06" /> but with the president and the prime minister publicly clashing regarding the state of the country.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2010-06-05 |title=Cavaco Silva e José Sócrates divergem na análise económica |url=https://arquivos.rtp.pt/conteudos/cavaco-silva-e-jose-socrates-divergem-na-analise-economica/ |access-date=2026-08-26 |website=RTP |language=pt}}</ref> 

===Pre-campaign===

In the follow-up for the presidential election, with Cavaco Silva having [[Opinion polling on the Aníbal Cavaco Silva presidency#First Term (2006–2011)|strong approval ratings]] and his re-election being virtually guaranteed,<ref>{{Cite web |date=2010-10-28 |title=Cavaco vence folgadamente as presidenciais |url=https://www.jn.pt/micro-sites/artigo/cavaco-vence-folgadamente-as-presidenciais/1697671 |access-date=2026-08-26 |website=Jornal de Notícias |language=pt}}</ref> divisions once again emerged in the Socialist Party regarding a candidate to face Cavaco, with the party fragmenting their votes between three candidates: the official party supported [[Manuel Alegre]], independent [[Fernando Nobre]], and party dissident, and former mayor of [[Viana do Castelo]], [[Defensor Moura]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2020-05-24 |title=Dramalhões presidenciais, essa bonita tradição socialista |url=https://eco.sapo.pt/especiais/dramalhoes-presidenciais-essa-bonita-tradicao-socialista/ |access-date=2026-08-26 |website=ECO |language=pt}}</ref> The [[Left Bloc]]'s support for Alegre also generated divisions and criticisms.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2011-01-19 |title=PS prepara-se para acusar BE se Alegre for derrotado  |url=https://www.publico.pt/2011/01/19/jornal/ps-preparase-para-acusar-be-se-alegre-for-derrotado-21051798 |access-date=2026-08-26 |website=Público |language=pt}}</ref> On the left-wing, the communists launched a relatively unknown candidate, party employee [[Francisco Lopes]],<ref>{{Cite web |date=2011-01-23 |title=Contra esquerda dividida, presidente tenta reeleição no 1º turno em Portugal |url=https://www.bbc.com/portuguese/noticias/2011/01/110123_portugal_eleicoesjair_pu |access-date=2026-08-26 |website=[[BBC News]] |language=pt}}</ref> while protest candidate [[José Manuel Coelho]] also filed a candidacy.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2010-12-29 |title=José Manuel Coelho é o sexto candidato a Belém |url=https://www.rtp.pt/noticias/politica/jose-manuel-coelho-e-o-sexto-candidato-a-belem_n402826 |access-date=2026-08-26 |website=RTP |language=pt}}</ref>

==Electoral system==
Any [[Portuguese nationality law|Portuguese citizen]] over 35 years old has the opportunity to run for president. In order to do so it is necessary to gather between 7,500 and 15,000 signatures and submit them to the [[Portuguese Constitutional Court]].

According to the [[Constitution of Portugal|Portuguese Constitution]], to be elected, a candidate needs a simple majority (50% + 1). If no candidate gets this majority there is a second round between the two most voted candidates. Changes to the electoral registration system, approved in 2008, which made registration automatic based on each voter's [[Identity card (Portugal)|Identity card]], led to a significant increase in the number of registered voters, from 9,1 million in 2006 to 9,7 million in 2011.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.publico.pt/2009/06/04/jornal/eles-nunca-foram-eleitores-e-gostavam--de-assim-ter-continuado-308863 |title=Eles nunca foram eleitores e gostavam de assim ter continuado |publisher=Público|access-date=14 July 2026|date=4 June 2009}}</ref>

